Create a concise, Git-ready Markdown triage and investigation guide for one detection.

INPUTS (use these values exactly; never reuse values from earlier guides in this chat)
- MITRE ATT&CK ID: [FILL IN]
- Technique Name: [FILL IN]
- Detection Name: [FILL IN]
- Detection logic screenshot: attached (optional). If attached, base the Detection Logic
  summary and the artifacts you reference on the logic shown. Do not make up field
  names, indexes or lookups.

AUDIENCE
Experienced Incident Response analysts. Keep it practical and concise. Do not give
long explanations.

AVAILABLE ANALYST TOOLS
- Splunk for host and network event logs
- Trellix Endpoint Security and Redline for endpoint triage and browser-history acquisition
- CrowdStrike Falcon, including Real-Time Response
- Out-of-band Windows and Linux VMs with open-source static and dynamic file-analysis tools

REQUIREMENTS
1. Use the exact heading structure in the template below.
2. Write suspicious or malicious artifacts as angle-bracket variables, such as
   `<hostname>`, `<username>`, `<process_name>`, `<command_line>`, `<file_path>`,
   `<file_hash>`, `<remote_domain>`, `<remote_ip_address>`, `<malicious_url>`.
   Do not use real atomic IOCs (URLs, hashes, IPs, domains, filenames, paths,
   usernames, hostnames). Native OS binaries, built-in tools and Event IDs that are
   part of the technique are not atomic IOCs and may be named.
3. Make every triage and investigation action specific to the technique.
4. Include validation steps for identifying approved or expected activity.
5. Include Splunk, Trellix/Redline, CrowdStrike Falcon and out-of-band analysis
   actions where they fit.
6. Include containment and escalation only when malicious activity is confirmed or
   strongly suspected.
7. Include realistic known false positives and malicious behaviors specific to the
   technique.
8. Include one concise example that contains no atomic IOCs.

GITHUB FORMATTING RULES
- Wrap every angle-bracket variable in backticks everywhere: headings, lists and the
  Example. GitHub removes a bare <variable> as HTML, so it would disappear.
- Text inside {{ }} in the template is an instruction for you. Replace it with real
  content. Never output the {{ }} or the instruction text.
- Write checklist items exactly as "- [ ] " (dash, space, brackets with a space
  inside, space).
- Put one blank line before and after every heading and every list.
- Do not use HTML tags, emojis or horizontal rules.
- Output the finished guide inside a single ````markdown code block, with nothing
  before or after it, so the raw Markdown can be pasted straight into GitHub.

